## Why This Is Asked
Tests pragmatic SSH latency debugging in multi-tenant contexts.

## Key Concepts
- sshd_config flags: UseDNS, GSSAPIAuthentication
- DNS resolution path and NSS order
- SSH debug with -vvv and performance timing
- Safe, minimal config changes with a clear rollback

## Code Example
```javascript
// Node.js snippet to measure SSH login latency (sanity-check)
const {exec} = require('child_process');
const start = Date.now();
exec('ssh -o BatchMode=yes user@host exit', (e, stdout, stder) => {
  const latency = Date.now() - start;
  console.log('latency_ms', latency);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- If latency persists after UseDNS=no, what’s next?
- How would you validate rollback and minimize user impact?

## Why This Is Asked
Handles a realistic, repeatable issue in multi-tenant Linux environments. Evaluates practical debugging, safe isolation, and rollback discipline.

## Key Concepts
- inotify and max_user_watches
- per-tenant resource control (ulimit, cgroups)
- observability and test planning
- safe rollback

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple Node.js watcher demo illustrating per-tenant file activity
const fs = require('fs');
const path = '/ci-scratch/tenant-A';
fs.watch(path, {recursive: true}, (ev, f) => {
  console.log(`[watch] ${ev}: ${f}`);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you automate per-tenant limit adjustments and verification?
- How would you validate rollback in a production window without impacting running builds?

